302:
Even though arising and ceasing are clearly present, because it is not easy to demonstrate arising and ceasing by distinguishing each of the rapidly changing phenomena, and because it is easy to demonstrate the arising of the rebirth-linking aggregates produced by various kammas that generate a new round of results (vipākavaṭṭa) without considering the arising and ceasing during the course of existence (pavatti), the Chapter on Arising (Uppādavāra) is taught solely by way of arising at the time of rebirth-linking. Because it is easy to demonstrate ceasing by the end of the generated round of results, the Chapter on Ceasing (Nirodhavāra) is taught by way of ceasing at the time of death.

What is the evidence for not considering arising and ceasing during the course of existence in this Khandha Yamaka? The Pali text itself is the evidence. Indeed, in the Pali, especially in the Future Portion of the Chapter on Arising, this Pali passage: "For those in their last birth, the aggregate of form will not arise, and the aggregate of feeling will not arise," is very clear evidence. Although it is appropriate for material and mental phenomena to arise during the course of existence for those in their last birth, because it is taught with the conclusion "the aggregate of form will not arise, and the aggregate of feeling will not arise," it should be understood that arising during the course of existence is not taken. This Pali passage: "For those attaining Parinibbāna in the Pure Abodes, the aggregate of perception has never ceased in that realm, but it is not that the aggregate of feeling does not cease for them in that realm," is also very clear evidence for not considering ceasing during the course of existence. For those attaining Parinibbāna in the Pure Abodes who are at the moment of dissolution (bhaṅgakkhaṇa) of the death-consciousness, the number of aggregates of perception that have arisen and ceased during the course of existence since rebirth-linking is beyond counting. Even though this is so, because it is taught with the conclusion "the aggregate of perception has never ceased for them in that realm," it should be understood that ceasing during the course of existence is not taken.

Thus, having understood the fixed characteristic of arising and ceasing in this context, and taking only the arising (uppāda) of rebirth-linking and the ceasing (bhaṅga) of death, the determination of the meaning of the answers that appear in various places should be understood. Since that determination of meaning can be understood in all answers by the method already stated in the initial answer, it is not elaborated upon in the sequence of answers. One who cannot understand the determination of meaning by the method thus given should understand it by approaching teachers and listening well.

